                                                                  7443 1451
RESOLVED RESONANCE PARAMETERS (L. Leal, ORNL, January 2008)       7443 1451
 Starting from the resonance parameter data in the IRDF-2002      7443 1451
 library, small adjustments were made to reproduce the thermal    7443 1451
 capture cross section of 38.09 barns and the resonance integral  7443 1451
 of 483.4 barns as closely as possible. This ratio 12.69 is in    7443 1451
 good agreement with the value 12.59+/-0.22 from the Kayzero      7443 1451
 database for neutron activation analysis [1].                    7443 1451
                                                                  7443 1451
RESOLVED RESONANCE PARAMETERS (IRDF-2002)                         7443 1451
    Resolved MLBW resonance parameters up to  8.5 keV  were based 7443 1451
on data from ref.[2-3]. Spins of resonances, which were not iden- 7443 1451
tified, have been assigned by taking into account 2*J+1 law  and  7443 1451
approximate linear growth of the number of levels with given spin 7443 1451
and parity from energy. Average gamma-widths 0.05 eV (L=0 and L=1)7443 1451
were assigned to those resonances, widths for which were absent.  7443 1451
Fictitious resonances were added in the energy region 4 - 8.5 keV 7443 1451
to obtain  the average capture cross section values  observed  in 7443 1451
the experiments [4],[5],[6],[7].                                  7443 1451
                                                                  7443 1451
UNRESOLVED RESONANCE PARAMETERS (IRDF-2002)                       7443 1451
    Unresolved resonance parameters in  the region  8.5 - 100 keV 7443 1451
were evaluated  with using 'EVPAR' code (Hauser-Feshbach-Moldauer 7443 1451
statistical model)[8]. They were calculated from optimized fit to 7443 1451
the measured capture [5,6,7,9,10] and total [11,12] cross sections7443 1451
data.  Average resolved resonance  parameters  have been  used as 7443 1451
zero approximation.  Contribution of  F-wave in the energy region 7443 1451
30 - 100 keV given as smoothed background cross section in File 3.7443 1451
                                                                  7443 1451
RESONANCE COVARIANCE DATA (L. Leal, ORNL, Jan 2008)               7443 1451
The procedure used to generate the resolved covariance data       7443 1451
with the SAMMY code is as follow:                                 7443 1451
 a) Retrieve best set of available resonance parameters.          7443 1451
 b) Convert the resonance parameters into the Reich-Moore form.   7443 1451
 c) Generate "experimental" cross sections for total, scattering, 7443 1451
    and capture cross sections with the NJOY code, for use as     7443 1451
    "experimental data" in SAMMY.                                 7443 1451
 d) Assign a global "experimental" uncertainty to the             7443 1451
    "experimental data." The uncertainties used were based on     7443 1451
    uncertainty in the thermal capture cross section and          7443 1451
    uncertainty in the capture resonance integrals.               7443 1451
 e) Run SAMMY code with the option to generate                    7443 1451
    resonance-covariances retroactively.                          7443 1451
 f) Convert the resonance-covariance results from SAMMY into      7443 1451
    the ENDF format for file 32 and MT=151.                       7443 1451
                                                                  7443 1451
The resulting resonance parameters and covariance were used in    7443 1451
the calculations of average cross sections and uncertainties.     7443 1451
The calculations were done with SAMMY, ERRORJ, and PUFF-IV codes. 7443 1451
The results of these three codes agreed very well.                7443 1451
                                                                  7443 1451
